WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.620
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:12.420
After an incredible day of Apple showcasing their&nbsp;
culmination of many-many amazing research papers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:18.720
and also light transport algorithms applied to&nbsp;
soundwaves. Today we are going to have a look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:27.180
at NVIDIA’s new invention that can play Minecraft,&nbsp;
but not in the way you think. It truly is a sight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:35.580
to behold as it is able to gather resources for&nbsp;
itself, go and mine, catch a fish, these things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:43.200
I expected. However, what I didn’t expect is&nbsp;
that can even engage in more complex tasks like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:49.320
building a base, or hunting this adorable&nbsp;
little piggy that kind of breaks my heart.

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:56.880
Now, it can do 3 more complex things, including&nbsp;
fighting, which I will show to you in a moment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:02.760
But let’s have a look at the paper, and&nbsp;
immediately when looking at the article,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:10.380
I was like “what?”. Are you sure? You see, this&nbsp;
paper claims to use large language models to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:17.160
play Minecraft. How is that even possible?&nbsp;
Large language models are typically adept at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:23.700
answering text-based questions with text-based&nbsp;
answers. So how does it play a computer game?

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:31.800
It doesn’t just play the game, but it plays it&nbsp;
incredibly well. How? Well, hold on to your papers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:39.480
Fellow Scholars, because it is able to build&nbsp;
a curriculum by itself, for itself. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:45.660
it gets some text information about the world&nbsp;
it is in, for instance, the items that it has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:53.700
or what time it is and who is nearby. And based&nbsp;
on this information, it starts reasoning by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.700 --> 00:02:00.480
itself. That is incredible. Look, for instance, if&nbsp;
we have a wooden pickaxe and some stones, then it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:07.680
is a good time to upgrade to a stone pickaxe. Or,&nbsp;
if it’s nighttime and there is a zombie nearby,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:15.480
it is time to grab a sword and shield. Now, these&nbsp;
are simple statements, but the key is that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:21.780
little AI makes these deductions by itself. We&nbsp;
don’t need to program these by hand, and thus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:27.540
if it were dropped into a completely different&nbsp;
game, it would likely do quite well there too.

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:35.100
But wait a minute, this is still just&nbsp;
text. How does it become gameplay? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:42.480
after creating this curriculum, the AI writes&nbsp;
computer code to control the game and achieve its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:49.500
goals. If this piece of code works, it observes&nbsp;
what it achieves, and then stores it away in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:55.080
a skill library. These will be the building&nbsp;
blocks, for new, more complex skills later.

00:02:55.080 --> 00:03:01.980
So a text-based chat assistant that&nbsp;
can play a video game. That is very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:05.340
impressive. So, how well can it play the game?

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:11.940
With a little help in the form of human&nbsp;
feedback, it can do three amazing things.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:19.080
One, it can build a house. And I have to&nbsp;
say, that is some proper craftsmanship there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:25.080
or proper craftsAIship. Lovely&nbsp;
house. Good job, little AI!

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:30.480
Two, it can also build a nether&nbsp;
portal. This allows it to travel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:35.220
to a different dimension with&nbsp;
unique terrain and resources.

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:42.420
And, get this, it can even fight an Enderman.&nbsp;
This creature makes short work of an unsuspecting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:50.340
player, however, the AI came well prepared with&nbsp;
high-quality equipment, and bam, we are done.

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:57.660
Now, these all sound good, but we are Fellow&nbsp;
Scholars here, so we are looking for a detailed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.660 --> 00:04:07.500
comparison of this technique against previous AIs.&nbsp;
Is this any better? Oh my goodness. Are you seeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:14.820
what I am seeing? It can explore so much more than&nbsp;
its predecessors, for instance, much more than the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:23.340
fan favorite AutoGPT, a version of ChatGPT that&nbsp;
can prompt itself and work on its own. And it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:33.480
gets better! Wow! Look at that. I can’t believe&nbsp;
it! It is more than 15 times faster than AutoGPT.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:41.700
AutoGPT has barely come out a few weeks ago, and&nbsp;
it has already been improved 15x already. Wow.

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:51.180
Just look at that! What took AutoGPT 75&nbsp;
iterations likely only takes maybe 5 iterations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:59.940
likely even less. AutoGPT plateaud at iron tools&nbsp;
where the new technique is barely getting warmed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:06.660
up. Incredible improvement in just a couple of&nbsp;
months. These text-based large language models&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.660 --> 00:05:13.680
can not only write code to play video games,&nbsp;
but they can even plan their journeys. And just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:19.740
imagine what they will be capable of just two more&nbsp;
